If home ranges are bivariate normals, the 95% home range radius is r = sqrt(5.99) * sigma. Then, the area of a circle is A = pi * r^2. So a sigma of 32 km yields a 95% home range size of 19270 km^2, nearly 3x larger than the home range size estimate you provided. If sigma was actually 6 km, you would get a 95% home range area of 677 km^2, in line with your estimate. I believe sigma is not identifiable given this trapping array, at least using an isotropic detection function.

Because animal movement is elongated along the major axis of the trapping array and the array is too narrow to encompass individual movement along the minor axis, it is possible that sigma might be identifiable under the anisotropic detection function model, but not the isotropic detection function model. If so, this could explain why the results do not make sense--the models are not comparable if sigma is substantially overestimated under isotropy. However, it's also possible (probable?) that sigma is not identifiable under either detection function model. The estimated sigmas under anisotropic space use also look too large.

Ben

